WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.068
-I want to talk about what's
going on in politics right now

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:04.704 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, we see
the current president is using

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:09.009 align:start size:94% position:6%
a lot of the levers of power
that are available to him

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:12.545 align:start size:94% position:6%
in taking steps,
hopefully in something

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:13.346 line:92%
as big as infrastructure.

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:15.715
He's getting checks in the
American people's pocket.

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:18.451
We obviously have a great
many people in this countr

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:21.788
in elected office
who speak ill of big government.

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:23.590 align:start size:94% position:6%
Would you make the case,
based on the time

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:24.357 line:92%
we're living in right now,

00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:28.161 align:start size:94% position:6%
that sometimes big governmen
is a helpful tool?

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:28.828 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-Sometimes it's helpful.

00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:32.465
It's always gonna be helpful
if it's properly directed.

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:34.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
But in a time like this,
it's essential.

00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:39.272 align:start size:94% position:6%
Look, don't forget, I gave you
the first balanced budget

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:41.508 align:start size:94% position:6%
in three decades,
and we did three in a row,

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:44.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I left a fourth,
and we paid $600 billion dow

00:00:44.411 --> 00:00:47.981 align:start size:94% position:6%
on the national debt,
which was real money back then

00:00:47.981 --> 00:00:50.350 line:92%
This is not that kind of time.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:53.086 size:84% position:42% line:92%
We had a crash in 2008,

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:55.555 align:start size:94% position:6%
which blew a $3 trillion hol
in the economy.

00:00:55.555 --> 00:01:00.326 align:start size:94% position:6%
Then we have the coronavirus
crash, which was bigger.

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:03.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
For all of the stimulus mone
that's been spent,

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:05.765 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're still about
3 million jobs short

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:09.169 align:start size:94% position:6%
of where we were
before this thing started.

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:12.272 align:start size:94% position:6%
When there is negative growt
and zero interest rates,

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:16.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
only the government
can provide the impetus

00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:17.877 line:92%
to jump-start the economy.

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:21.414
And since we're doing it,
the President has a plan

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:25.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
to try to shape the future
with modernized infrastructure

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:28.021 align:start size:94% position:6%
including broadband
for all these small towns

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:29.122 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
in rural areas.

00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:31.925 align:start size:94% position:6%
And a lot of transfer
to clean energy.

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:34.094 align:start size:94% position:6%
But things that
are generating jobs.

00:01:34.094 --> 00:01:36.229 align:start size:94% position:6%
And a big plan
to help small businesses.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:40.633 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if we hadn't done that
God only knows how many

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:44.204
million people would have been
thrown out of their apartments

00:01:44.204 --> 00:01:46.506 align:start size:94% position:6%
Too many lost their jobs
as it was.

00:01:46.506 --> 00:01:48.108 line:92%
So, yeah, I think they --

00:01:48.108 --> 00:01:51.344
You know, people need to jus
park this cussin' the government

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:53.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
for a while,
and let's get the thing

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:56.249 align:start size:94% position:6%
back to ground zero
and start growing again.

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:58.551
Then we can argue about how much
the government should do.

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:02.288 size:84% position:42%
-And it does seem to be
the discussion du jour

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:04.691 align:start size:94% position:6%
in American politics is,
what would you do

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:10.063 size:75% position:38%
about the filibuster
if it was up to you?

00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:11.898 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Well...

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:18.171
the filibuster was set up
to ensure prolonged debate

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:21.474 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was used
in the Civil Rights era

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:24.611 align:start size:94% position:6%
to try to kill
civil rights bills.

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:29.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
And starting
in about 1993, or '4,

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:31.985 align:start size:94% position:6%
it started to be used
all the time.

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:34.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was used
on President Obama.

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:42.829
So it's now been used to try
to prevent a repeat of 202

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:46.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
when we had the enormous
voter participation.

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:50.703 align:start size:94% position:6%
So all the states that are
in the hands of people

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:54.207 align:start size:94% position:6%
who don't like that
and want to maintain

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:58.178 line:92%
a racial income differential

00:02:58.178 --> 00:02:59.379 align:start size:94% position:6%
are trying to make it
harder to vote.

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:03.349
I would -- I understand
the President's reluctant

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:06.519 align:start size:94% position:6%
to get rid of it altogether,
and I sympathize

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:08.922 align:start size:94% position:6%
with Joe Manchin,
who's trying to, you know,

00:03:08.922 --> 00:03:11.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
stand up for the right thing
and represent people

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:14.861 align:start size:94% position:6%
that voted more than 2-1
for President Trump,

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:17.530
but I think, when it comes
to preserving democracy,

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.466 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, I would suspend
the filibuster,

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:21.534 line:92%
because I think it's essential

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:24.270 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't think that we should
be in the business

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:29.509
of going backwards in trying
to drive down voting rights.

00:03:29.509 --> 00:03:30.643 line:92%
I think we need to expand it

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:33.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think that every tim
we've done it before,

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:35.615 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's made us a better country,
made us a stronger country

00:03:35.615 --> 00:03:40.253
And it'll do both parties good
to have to get out and compete

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:42.989 line:92%
for all these new voters.

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:46.893 size:84% position:42%
So, yes, if it were me,
for this one purpose,

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:50.296 line:92%
I would not allow an expansion

00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:51.931 align:start size:94% position:6%
and basically the protection
of voting rights.

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:57.437
These guys are trying to take us
back decades in voting access,

00:03:57.437 --> 00:04:01.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
the people that
Senator McConnell represents

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.777
-James, you've had in your books
the president's gone missing

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:06.379 align:start size:94% position:6%
the president's daughter's
gone missing.

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:08.181 align:start size:94% position:6%
I sense a theme.
You know, if there's gonna b

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:10.617 align:start size:94% position:6%
a trilogy of books from
you guys, what do you think?

00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:13.519 align:start size:94% position:6%
-People really like animals,
so, you know.

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:14.754 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:18.124 size:78% position:39%
-It does feel like...
-Puppies. Puppies.

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:19.993
-Puppies. The president.
Maybe it's a whole litter.

00:04:19.993 --> 00:04:22.996
The president's dog has a
litter, and they all go missing.

00:04:22.996 --> 00:04:23.930 line:92%
-Yeah, yeah, you never know.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.599 size:88% position:44%
-The president's family
wants him to go missing.

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:28.801 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
Will he do that?

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:30.270 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We're in talks.
We're in talks.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:31.471 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You're in talks.
When do you know --

00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:34.007
Last question -- when do
you know the book is done?

00:04:34.007 --> 00:04:35.141 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, when you guys
are working on it,

00:04:35.141 --> 00:04:38.444 size:81% position:41%
is it your job to say,
as the expert, like,

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:41.314 align:start size:94% position:6%
"This is it.
We've got it. Send it off"

00:04:41.314 --> 00:04:42.882 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Once again,
it's just never come up,

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:45.885 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the other thing
that the President does is

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:49.155 align:start size:94% position:6%
he really insists
that the characters are as dee

00:04:49.155 --> 00:04:52.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
as we can make them,
as complex, rounded characters

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:55.662
And that's probably the last
thing that we do, in terms of,

00:04:55.662 --> 00:04:58.431 align:start size:94% position:6%
"All right,
this villain is a cliché

00:04:58.431 --> 00:05:00.566 line:92%
rather than a real human being."

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:02.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's probably
the last thing we do.

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:05.605 size:84% position:42%
And at a certain point,
I just go, "I give up.

00:05:05.605 --> 00:05:07.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's over.
It's done. It's done."

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:09.575 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then there it is --
"The President's Daughter.

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, congratulations.
-Thanks.

00:05:11.544 --> 00:05:13.780 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I do think both of you --
not just one of you --

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:16.416 align:start size:94% position:6%
both of you are going places
[ Laughter ]

00:05:16.416 --> 00:05:19.218 align:start size:94% position:6%
"The President's Daughter"
is out now.

